emographic effects [83…85]. To detect such effec\ts, we used 10-kb nonoverlapping windows of the ratio of United States/\(Africa Madagascar\) pnt to identify regions of the genome showing a disproportionate reduction of variation in the US sample \(Materials and Methods\). )>>endobj2671 0 obj<</Count 2/Dest[40 0 R/XYZ 45 327 null]/First 3045 0 R/Last 3046 0 R/Next 3041 0 R/Parent 2667 0 R/Prev 2668 0 R/SE 814 0 R/Title(summarize the magnitude of large-scale, multilocus linkage disequilibrium for each window \(Figure S3\). Use of the chimeric SIM4/6 assembly may reduce our power to detect unusual genomic regions but should not lead us to mistakenly identify such regi\ons. At least two salient points emerged from this analysis. First, large regions of the genome showing a severely reduced heterozygosity-to-divergence ratio, such as the tip of the X chromosome, tend to have high levels of linkage disequilibrium. Second,\ som)>>endobj3045 0 obj<</Dest[40 0 R/XYZ 285 202 null]/Next 3046 0 R/Parent 2671 0 R/SE 2139 0 R/Title(-)>>endobj3046 0 obj<</Dest[40 0 R/XYZ 285 191 null]/Parent 2671 0 R/Prev 3045 0 R/SE 2140 0 R/Title(-)>>endobj3044 0 obj<</Dest[40 0 R/XYZ 285 66 null]/Parent 3042 0 R/SE 2145 0 R/Title(-)>>endobj2665 0 obj<</Count 1/Dest[44 0 R/XYZ 306 165 null]/First 3047 0 R/Last 3047 0 R/Next 2661 0 R/Parent 2659 0 R/Prev 3048 0 R/SE 829 0 R/Title(For polarized MK tests, we used the D. yakuba genome to infer which “xed differences between D. simulans and D. melanogaster occurred along the D. simulans lineage \(Materials and Methods\). These “xations were then compared to D. simulans polymorphisms. \This reduced, “ltered dataset contained 2,676 genes of which 384 \(14%\) and 169 \(6%\) were signi“cant at the 0.05 and 0.01 levels, respectively \(deviating in the direction of adaptive evolution; Datasets S1\). Twenty-three genes showed evidence of a si\gni“cant \()>>endobj3047 0 obj<</Dest[44 0 R/XYZ 546 121 null]/Parent 2665 0 R/SE 2060 0